# Paths whose review is reserved to a specific role, rather than any reviewer.
#
# GitHub requires the "Require review from Code Owners" branch-protection rule for
# these entries to be enforced; without it they only auto-request the reviewer.
#
# The owner below is a placeholder. Point every rule at your fork's owning role with:
#
#   make setup-replace-codeowners OWNERS='@example-org/tech-leads'

# Supply-chain controls. The npm `min-release-age` cooldown in each .npmrc is
# evaluated only while npm resolves dependencies, so a lockfile produced with it
# switched off installs cleanly under `npm ci` and shows no symptom in CI.
# Overriding the cooldown is a deliberate call — reacting to a CRITICAL advisory is
# a legitimate one — and this entry is what reserves that call to the owning role.
# `npm-cooldown-audit.yaml` detects and reports a bypass but never blocks it; this
# is the half that enforces.
#
# pnpm states the same policy in pnpm-workspace.yaml (minimumReleaseAge and the
# strictness flags around it, plus allowBuilds, blockExoticSubdeps and trustPolicy).
# It refuses at resolution time rather than warning afterwards, so there is no
# audit tool watching that half — the settings file itself is the control, which
# makes review of it the only thing standing between a weakened window and a
# lockfile nobody questions.
#
# The tool cooldown states the override differently again. The entries above are
# settings and lockfiles where a weakened window has to be inferred from what the
# file no longer refuses; `tool-cooldown-bypass.toml` is the override written out
# in full, one dated entry per tool taken early. That makes it the easiest of these
# to read and the easiest to add a line to, which is why the reservation matters
# here rather than mattering less.
**/.npmrc                             @Tomy-ch
**/package-lock.json                  @Tomy-ch
**/pnpm-lock.yaml                     @Tomy-ch
**/pnpm-workspace.yaml                @Tomy-ch
.github/actions-pin.toml              @Tomy-ch
.github/tool-cooldown-bypass.toml     @Tomy-ch
docker/images-pin.toml                @Tomy-ch

# Go modules have no toolchain-side cooldown to bypass — nothing refuses a
# freshly published version, so nothing can detect that one was taken. Review is
# the only control on this ecosystem, which makes the entry below load-bearing
# rather than symmetrical bookkeeping.
go.mod                                @Tomy-ch
go.sum                                @Tomy-ch
